What we know is that the AFP has been working very closely with the Indonesians on counter-terrorism issues since the Bali bombing of 2002. This led to the creation of the Jakarta Operations Centre, later known as the Jakarta Regional Cooperation Team, in 2002.
The AFP's largest overseas contingent remains in the one in Indonesia, where 30 officers are based. Moreover, at the same time as the Bali drug smugglers were arrested in 2005, the AFP was establishing a Multinational Operations Support Team in Semarang, Indonesia.
By all accounts, these operations have been highly successful. This is attested to by Foreign Minister Julie Bishop accidentally mentioning the AFP's "security intelligence" work with the Indonesian Police in an ABC radio interview earlier this year.
Indonesia is the most populous Muslim country in the world, and it is a short boat ride from Australia.
