Auburn defensive back Stephen Roberts, who was arrested last week on misdemeanor charges for an unlicensed firearm and attempting eluding of an officer, is suspended for Saturday's season opener against No. 2 Clemson and possibly longer.
Auburn coach Gus Malzahn announced the punishment for Roberts, who was arrested during a traffic stop last Wednesday night in Auburn, during his weekly press conference on Tuesday.
"We will be playing without Stephen Roberts," Malzahn said. "Stephen's going to be suspended for this game.... It's going to be week to week. We'll see what happens. We'll see how he responds."
Roberts was "a passenger in a vehicle and fled from officers during a traffic stop," according to Auburn Police Capt. Lorenza Dorsey, who said the firearm in Roberts' possession was a handgun.
Both charges are Class A misdemeanors in Alabama and carry a penalty of no more than one year in jail and up to a $6,000 fine. Roberts is due in court at 8 a.m. on Nov. 17. The Opelika native was released on bond of $2,000 later Wednesday night.
"Minor setback for a major comeback," Roberts wrote in a tweet Saturday afternoon.
Minor setback for a major comeback [?] [?] [?] [?] [?] -- ghostboy_4 (@stephenisland_4) August 27, 2016
Malzahn said Auburn's team policy is for players not to have handguns, licensed or not.
"We don't want them," Malzahn said. "We don't want our players with them."
However, multiple now-former players have posted pictures to social media of themselves using firearms for recreational purposes, typically for hunting or target practice, during Malzahn's tenure as head coach.
The last prior gun-related incident with an Auburn football player prior to Roberts' arrest last week was the fatal shooting of Jakell Mitchell over two and a half years ago. Mitchell, who was also from Opelika and was roommates with Roberts during the fall of 2014, had a.45-caliber Glock handgun when he exchanged fire with Markale Hart while attending a party at an Auburn apartment complex in Dec. 2014.
Roberts is not listed on Auburn's depth chart for the season opener, which was released Tuesday.
A 5-foot-11, 183-pound junior, Roberts was expected to start at free safety for the Tigers this season after he closed last year with four straight starts. Defensive coordinator Kevin Steele listed Roberts among the team's top four safeties and among the players taking reps at cornerback, where Auburn lost redshirt-freshman Jamel Dean, possibly for the season, due to a knee injury during fall camp.
On Sunday, Clemson coach Dabo Swinney offered no comment on the likelihood Auburn would be without Roberts during the season opener.
"I don't have any reaction to that," Swinney said. "I'm just focused really on our guys."
