Jimmy Anderson, an artist given a canvas that most of his kind would abhor, was quite brilliant on Friday evening. On a sluggish, energy-sapping track, where the ball habitually declined to bounce above knee-high, he dispatched India's three most experienced batsmen to give England the prospect of winning this Test rather than merely hanging on for the draw. At the close India were 87 for four in reply to England's laborious but distinctly useful 330.
This is no terror track. In fact it often seems more frightening to bowlers than batsmen. It is a slow burner rather than a slow turner. Yet Anderson was capable of undermining India's efforts to stay in this series as well as hastening the termination of Sachin Tendulkar's Test career.
In his first over Anderson removed Virender Sehwag. There was a hint of in-swing with the new ball; even so a startled Sehwag played inside the line whereupon his middle stump went missing. Then after Graeme Swann – this time a beneficiary of the absence of DRS – had dismissed Cheteshwar Pujara, given out caught at short leg, Anderson was recalled to bowl at Tendulkar.
He needed only three balls to dismiss him for the ninth time in his career – no one has dismissed Tendulkar as often and the other contenders may well be running out of time. The decisive ball jagged into Tendulkar before brushing the inside edge of his bat and knocking back the middle stump.
The crowd was hushed – soon many of them would leave the stadium – and in deep contemplation Tendulkar headed off to the distant pavilion. It felt as if we may have just witnessed his penultimate knock in Test cricket. Then Anderson found a flicker of away movement and the outside edge of Gautam Gambhir's bat. For the home side there were not enough plasters for a haemorrhaging batting lineup.
Joe Root had provided the glue for England in the first part of the day. No matter who bowled he offered a straight, calm bat and he bided his time like an old pro. His judgment was so sure that he could have dispensed with his left pad. He always used his bat.
This calmness against spin, which he had demonstrated in the nets and in his century for the development squad in Mumbai, obviously made a big impression on the tour management before this Test. It transpires that Alastair Cook had been especially impressed by Root and he was the man to open up the idea of a change in England's lineup, which no one expected.
